window.GG_STORIES = {"v":1,"made":"2026-11-21","k":{"v":1,"round":"s1","folder":"t"},"list":[{"id":"s002","slug":"birth-plan","title":"Birth Plan","sub":"She spent thirty years catching people on their way in.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/birth-plan","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Birth Plan · Read it on Grounded","mins":9,"preview":["\"Why am I still here?\" Jan cried out from her wheelchair in the corner of the dining room. She repeated herself, sharper this time, in case anyone was confused: \"Why am I still alive?\"","The room went quiet, and I could feel every eye on me, waiting to see how I'd answer.","This was my second visit with Jan. In our first visit she told me about the thirty years she spent walking babies into the world, night shifts in the labor and delivery unit, then home by morning to raise her own three kids. Nothing but good memories that day.","But a lot had changed for Jan in the last month. Her quality of life had been slipping, week by week.","Still, I was caught off guard. It was almost like she'd had the question loaded, just waiting for me to walk in.","She'd asked me the rawest question there is, straight from the heart, and I wanted to meet it with something just as honest. Not here, though. Not with the whole dining room listening. I thought fast, looking for a way to get us somewhere private.","I let my serious face crack into a grin and said, to Jan and everyone else listening, \"Well? The good Lord must have something fun planned for ya, Jan!\"","I laid it on with a southern drawl I don't usually have. Jan's whole face lit up, and a few chuckles went around the room.","I took the opening, stood, and turned her chair toward the hallway. \"Let's go talk about this, Jan. Maybe we can ask the Lord directly and figure it out.\""],"posted":"","topics":["dying"]},{"id":"s004","slug":"drift-away","title":"Drift Away","sub":"Some people don't say goodbye. They sing it.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/drift-away","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Drift Away · Read it on Grounded","mins":5,"preview":["Suzan had not spoken in three days.","But I knew her voice. I had been visiting her for the better part of a month, and in those early visits she had plenty to say. She told me about her husband, the long happy kind of marriage that people do not talk about enough. She told me about raising her children, about the grandchildren who came after, about the particular joy of watching your kids become parents. She had a warm and uncomplicated faith, the kind that does not need to argue with anyone. She loved God and she loved her small country church and she loved the people in it. There was nothing restless about her belief. It fit her the way a good coat fits, worn in and reliable and exactly right.","Then the cancer finished what it had started and the words stopped coming."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying"]},{"id":"s005","slug":"enlightenment","title":"Enlightenment","sub":"Sixty years of prayer to reach it. Now he wanted company for what was left of the walk.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/enlightenment","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Enlightenment · Read it on Grounded","mins":8,"preview":["Matthew is a Catholic monk in his eighties, a religious scholar who has spent decades in centered prayer, contemplation, and solitude. He came to this monastery at eighteen, fresh out of high school, and never left. He's been my hospice patient a few weeks now, one of five retired monks living out their days in a wing the order converted for hospice care, inside walls built for something older and holier.","He met me in the doorway, hunched hard over a cane built for a man a foot taller than him, one hand braced against the frame. But that smile of his took up more of his face than a frame that small should have allowed.","\"How are you feeling today, Matthew?\""],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying","faith"]},{"id":"s006","slug":"grief-debt","title":"Grief Debt","sub":"","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/grief-debt","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Grief Debt · Read it on Grounded","mins":4,"preview":["Some weeks just feel heavier than others.","This week I caught myself walking around numb. Not sad, not angry, just numb. That is usually my first clue that I have racked up too much grief debt.","Grief debt is what happens when life keeps handing you losses and you keep telling yourself you will process them later. The emotions pile up quietly like laundry you swear you are going to fold. Eventually the pile gets big enough that it starts tripping you when you walk through the room."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["grief"]},{"id":"s007","slug":"grounded-in-coffee","title":"Grounded in Coffee","sub":"","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":true,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/grounded-in-coffee","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Grounded in Coffee · Read it on Grounded","mins":4,"preview":["The other day I was having one of those rough starts we all know too well. Everything already felt a little heavier than usual.","I was heading out on the highway to my first visit when a reckless driver came flying past and cut right in front of me. I slammed on the brakes. My work bag flew forward and knocked my full coffee cup across the seat. The outside of the car was fine. The inside? Total disaster, coffee everywhere.","Of course, the same driver ended up stopped at the red light just ahead of me. I pulled up beside him and gave him my best stink eye. He rolled down his window and started yelling at me like it was my fault.","In that moment, I had to remind myself: You are a hospice chaplain on your way to care for patients and families. You cannot do this. I kept my eyes forward, left the window up, and let him speed away. But my whole body was shaking with adrenaline."],"posted":"","topics":[],"full":["The other day I was having one of those rough starts we all know too well. Everything already felt a little heavier than usual.","I was heading out on the highway to my first visit when a reckless driver came flying past and cut right in front of me. I slammed on the brakes. My work bag flew forward and knocked my full coffee cup across the seat. The outside of the car was fine. The inside? Total disaster, coffee everywhere.","Of course, the same driver ended up stopped at the red light just ahead of me. I pulled up beside him and gave him my best stink eye. He rolled down his window and started yelling at me like it was my fault.","In that moment, I had to remind myself: You are a hospice chaplain on your way to care for patients and families. You cannot do this. I kept my eyes forward, left the window up, and let him speed away. But my whole body was shaking with adrenaline.","I had only a few minutes before my first visit, and I needed to show up calm, present, and actually useful to the family I was about to meet. So I pulled over near a quiet field, took a deep breath, and used the 5-4-3-2-1 grounding technique right there in the driver’s seat.","It goes like this:Five things you can see.Four things you can feel.Three things you can hear.Two things you can smell.One thing you can taste.","I named five things I could see: my work bag, the blue sky, the golden field, my hands gripping the wheel, and the spilled coffee on the floor.Four things I could feel: my feet on the floor mat, the fabric of my clothes, the seat against my back, and the warmth of the coffee still soaking into my pants.Three sounds: the hum of the engine, traffic whooshing by, and my own breathing finally beginning to slow.Two smells: spilled coffee and the crisp morning air coming through the cracked window.And finally, one taste: more of that coffee.","As I finished the exercise, I had to laugh out loud. I was officially grounded in coffee. Four out of five senses, all coffee.","That moment has stayed with me. In this work, and really in all of life, things spill. Plans fall apart. People cut us off (sometimes literally). Our bodies react before our minds can catch up. The question is always: how do we come back to center quickly so we can still show up for the people who need us?","Sometimes the tools are simple. Sometimes the mess itself becomes part of the medicine.","What about you? Have you ever had one of those mornings (or whole seasons) where everything felt like it was spilling over and you had to find your way back to grounded? I’d love to hear your stories in the comments.","Thanks for reading."]},{"id":"s008","slug":"he-came-to-collect","title":"He Came to Collect","sub":"What a dying woman's refusal taught me about love","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/he-came-to-collect","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, He Came to Collect · Read it on Grounded","mins":6,"preview":["Evelyn looks nothing like someone who is dying.","She is bright-eyed, quick with a laugh, always dressed like she is expecting company. She joins card games in the sunroom, sings along at music hour, and keeps a running tally of who is winning at bingo. On paper she is on hospice. You would never know it.","I visit her every couple of weeks at the memory care facility that has become her home. To me she is the woman who grew up on a Midwest farm with three brothers and three sisters, who chased chickens and rode tractors with her dad and once found barn kittens in her school lunch pail. She is not religious in any formal way, but she loves talking about God the way a neighbor might speak of an old friend they have not seen in years but still think about fondly."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying","kids"]},{"id":"s009","slug":"he-deserves-that","title":"He Deserves That","sub":"A sister's last phone call, and the room where the veil went thin","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/he-deserves-that","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, He Deserves That · Read it on Grounded","mins":10,"preview":["What started as a routine phone call quickly escalated into something else.","It was the end of my day and I was calling new admits to introduce myself and ask about spiritual care.","\"Hi Diane, my name is Chris. I'm one of the chaplains. Is now an okay time to talk?\""],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying","faith"]},{"id":"s010","slug":"he-was-praying-too","title":"He Was Praying Too","sub":"On miracles, doubt, and showing up anyway","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/he-was-praying-too","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, He Was Praying Too · Read it on Grounded","mins":10,"preview":["The pager went off right after dinner with my family.","I looked down: 66-year-old male, motorcycle accident, life flight.","I kissed my family goodbye and drove."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s011","slug":"in-the-beginning","title":"In the Beginning","sub":"A chaplain, a woman with dementia, and the words that stayed.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":true,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":5,"preview":["I sat in the parking lot longer than I needed to.","Not because I was dreading the visit. I liked Ruth. I had been seeing her for over a year and she was warm and bright eyed and always happy to see me. The problem was I had started to feel like I was not really doing anything. I would go in, we would chat, she would say things that did not quite connect, I would smile and follow along, and then I would leave. A social call dressed up as chaplaincy.","Ruth had advanced dementia. Before the disease she had been deeply rooted in her faith, active in her church for decades, the kind of woman who knew her Bible the way some people know their neighborhood, every street, every shortcut, every house that mattered. But whenever I tried to read Scripture to her she would look at me with a polite blank expression, like I was speaking a language she almost recognized. When I prayed she would stare back, pleasant but somewhere else entirely.","So I had settled into the habit of just being present, mirroring her energy, following her into whatever world she was living in that day. It was not nothing. But it was not enough.","I said a quiet prayer in the car before I got out. Something like, let me actually reach her today. Then I went in."],"posted":"2026-10-14T12:57:30.759Z","topics":["faith"],"full":["I sat in the parking lot longer than I needed to.","Not because I was dreading the visit. I liked Ruth. I had been seeing her for over a year and she was warm and bright eyed and always happy to see me. The problem was I had started to feel like I was not really doing anything. I would go in, we would chat, she would say things that did not quite connect, I would smile and follow along, and then I would leave. A social call dressed up as chaplaincy.","Ruth had advanced dementia. Before the disease she had been deeply rooted in her faith, active in her church for decades, the kind of woman who knew her Bible the way some people know their neighborhood, every street, every shortcut, every house that mattered. But whenever I tried to read Scripture to her she would look at me with a polite blank expression, like I was speaking a language she almost recognized. When I prayed she would stare back, pleasant but somewhere else entirely.","So I had settled into the habit of just being present, mirroring her energy, following her into whatever world she was living in that day. It was not nothing. But it was not enough.","I said a quiet prayer in the car before I got out. Something like, let me actually reach her today. Then I went in.","The memory care unit smelled like lunch being prepared somewhere down the hall. I found Ruth in the common room in her favorite chair, a worn blue cardigan buttoned up to her chin, watching a game show with the focused expression of someone who has no idea what is happening but is committed to finding out. She lit up when she saw me.","We went back to her room. The walls were covered in family photos, generations of them. We settled in and talked for a while, or she talked and I listened and reflected back, which is how it usually went.","Then something nudged me.","\"Okay,\" I said, leaning forward a little. \"I am going to read something to you. Tell me if it sounds familiar.\"","She smiled. \"Okay!\"","I opened my Bible and started reading. In the beginning.","Ruth's face changed completely. Her eyes went wide and then soft and then she laughed out loud, the real laugh, the one I had not heard before.","\"Oh jeez,\" she said, \"I know that one, silly!\"","I almost fell out of my chair.","I kept reading, just a verse or two at a time, pausing between each one. Light. Sky. Water. Land. Sun and moon and stars. Ruth tracked every word. I could see something moving behind her eyes, some door opening that had been stuck a long time. These were not new words to her. These were the first words. Sunday school words. Little girl words, learned so early they had been woven into her before the disease had a chance to find them.","When I finished she sat quietly for a moment. Then she looked at me and said, \"That is beautiful.\"","I did not push further. I did not need to. Something had already opened that did not need explaining, and I have learned not to walk back through a door like that trying to prop it wider.","Within a few minutes she had drifted back into her usual world, telling me about work she needed to finish and children she needed to get home to. I sat with her there too. Wherever she was, I wanted her to know someone was with her.","Before I left I said a short prayer of thanks. She bowed her head like she had been doing it her whole life.","Because she had.","She did not remember my name when I left. She probably did not remember the visit.","But for a few minutes she remembered something better."]},{"id":"s012","slug":"if-she-is-still-here","title":"If She Is Still Here","sub":"I almost said tomorrow. She didn't have a tomorrow.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/if-she-is-still-here","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, If She Is Still Here · Read it on Grounded","mins":7,"preview":["\"It's mom. She's taken a turn and not doing well. Can you come?\"","I was off that day, and my work phone was supposed to be off too. It never is. Not because I plan on answering everything that comes through at all hours, but in case a message like this one shows up, and I need to.","I had been visiting this family for about a year. Every visit started and ended with a hug, the kind that happens naturally once you've spent enough time with people to stop being a visitor.","I texted back: \"I'm sorry to hear this. I'm off today but I can come by tomorrow.\"","\"Okay,\" she replied. \"If she is still here.\"","My heart dropped. I'd been with Carol only a few days earlier, telling silly jokes, admiring her latest diamond art project. I had no idea she'd declined this fast.","I typed back fast. \"I can come now if that's okay?\"","\"Thank you.\" That was the only reply.","I grabbed my things and was out the door within minutes. Thirty minutes later I was standing at a front door I'd knocked on more than a dozen times.","The house was always the same. Grandkids' toys lined up neatly in the living room. Cozy furniture arranged to catch the light from the big window. Art on every wall, seasonal decor changed out like clockwork."],"posted":"","topics":["dying","grief"]},{"id":"s013","slug":"love","title":"Love","sub":"A Connection Words Could Never Carry","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/love","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Love · Read it on Grounded","mins":7,"preview":["Kathy was glowing.","I noticed it from forty feet away, before I even reached her table.","Some mornings I get a pull toward a name on my caseload, and I can't explain it. No report, no reason. Just a need to go. That's what brought me to her memory care unit today."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s014","slug":"my-boundaries-have-gates","title":"My Boundaries Have Gates","sub":"","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/my-boundaries-have-gates","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, My Boundaries Have Gates · Read it on Grounded","mins":5,"preview":["Some mornings hit different before I even leave the house. That day started with a flood of messages, but one line stopped me cold: “Decline. Daily nursing visits. Family has been notified and is coping.”","I had to get there before it was too late.","On our previous visit, when I told him I’d see him again the next week, he looked at me with that half-joking, half-desperate smile and said, “You promise, right?”"],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying","grief"]},{"id":"s015","slug":"please-help-my-dad-die","title":"Please Help My Dad Die","sub":"Sometimes the hardest part of dying is finding permission to leave","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/please-help-my-dad-die","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Please Help My Dad Die · Read it on Grounded","mins":7,"preview":["I was recently called to the bedside of a man who was actively dying. At admission to hospice, he had declined a chaplain outright. He wasn't religious, he said, and he wanted nothing to do with spiritual care. All I really knew was that he was struggling to let go, and his daughter hoped I might be able to help.","These moments come more often than you'd think. The details change, but the ache is familiar: someone hanging on, tethered by unfinished business. I never know quite what I'll find, so I simply show up, open, and listen.","I arrived at his daughter's well-kept home, filled with antiques and stories. It had been gently transformed into a makeshift hospice room. Hospital bed in the front living room. Walker and wheelchair tucked to the side. The ever-present bedside commode standing sentinel."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying","kids"]},{"id":"s016","slug":"prayer","title":"Prayer","sub":"A Language Dementia Cannot Touch","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/thank-god-for-sending-you","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Prayer · Read it on Grounded","mins":10,"preview":["Trash caught in the shrubs by the entrance. My heart sank a little before I even reached the door.","Inside, the lobby smelled like urine. I hadn't even reached the unit yet.","I sat by a small glass window and waited for someone to buzz me in. A young staff member finally came, read my badge, and gave me a short, flat look. She didn't say anything. She didn't have to."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s017","slug":"the-atypical-atheist","title":"The Atypical Atheist","sub":"How one visit broke through years of religious distrust","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/the-atypical-atheist","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, The Atypical Atheist · Read it on Grounded","mins":4,"preview":["I missed the note. Classic me.","I walked into the group home ready for a visit and the man took one look at my badge and exploded. \"I don't want a damn chaplain. I don't believe in any of that bs.\"","I smiled, hands open and calm. \"Totally fine. We don't have to talk about any of that bs if you don't want to. So tell me, what do you believe in?\""],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s019","slug":"the-impossible-dance-of-particles","title":"The Impossible Dance of Particles","sub":"A brilliant physicist rediscovers faith through her microscope","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/the-impossible-dance-of-particles","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, The Impossible Dance of Particles · Read it on Grounded","mins":3,"preview":["I met her in a quiet corner of the nursing home. The staff adored her, but when hospice arrived she immediately waved off any offer to see a chaplain. “Not religious,” she said with finality.","Fair enough.","Then the difficult days came. Pain clawed at her body and the reality of her situation pressed in. A nurse gently asked again if she would talk to someone. “Just to talk,” she said. “No religion.” The woman sighed deeply and agreed."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s020","slug":"the-recovery","title":"The Recovery","sub":"What happened after mattered more than the mistake.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/the-recovery","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, The Recovery · Read it on Grounded","mins":11,"preview":["I broke a dying woman's saucer yesterday. It is not the breaking that stays with me. It is everything that happened after.","Gail lives in a locked memory care unit, in a small room with one window, a big TV, a hospital bed, and a china cabinet. The walls are mostly bare, just a few photographs. I have been visiting her for a few months now. She is easygoing, keeps mostly to herself, and her dementia makes real conversation more work than reward some days, more frustration than connection. Early on, our visits were quiet. I would sit near her recliner, she would drift, and I never knew exactly where I stood with her.","Then one afternoon I noticed the china cabinet."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":[]},{"id":"s021","slug":"total-bliss","title":"Total Bliss","sub":"Not afraid. Not even close. Just eager.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/total-bliss","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Total Bliss · Read it on Grounded","mins":5,"preview":["\"How do you handle all the death and dying?\"","People ask me this more than anything else. Young chaplain me used to wonder the same thing, quietly, on the drive home some nights. I have an answer now, and it still catches people off guard: most of the deaths I witness are not the tragedy everyone braces for. There is no wailing. No suffering. There is reminiscing, life's most cherished moments told one more time. There is gratitude, the kind that surprises even the person feeling it. There is a closeness to God I have never found anywhere else, right where the veil between life and death goes thin.","Jane taught me that better than almost anyone.","I met her in an assisted living facility a few days before she died. Her room was small and full of her, photographs crowding every flat surface, a quilt someone had clearly made by hand folded at the foot of the bed. Afternoon light came through the blinds in stripes across the covers. She had been a young widow, left to raise two children alone after her husband Gary died overseas. She told me how, in the worst of that grief, God had promised her He would take care of her and her children.","\"Did you ever remarry?\" I asked."],"posted":"","topics":["dying"]},{"id":"s022","slug":"welcome-home","title":"Welcome Home","sub":"On ancestors, thresholds, and the ones who come to walk us home.","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"https://chri5j0y.substack.com/p/welcome-home","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, Welcome Home · Read it on Grounded","mins":6,"preview":["\"Dad is seeing ghosts. What does this mean?\"","The text came in before I'd taken my first sip of coffee, and I already knew what it meant. I didn't want to be the one to say it over text, so I wrote back, \"We might be getting close. Can I come by?\"","\"Yes.\""],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying"]},{"id":"s081","slug":"sarah","title":"Sarah","sub":"Every single ordinary day was actually extraordinary.","col":"The Wisdom They Share","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":3,"preview":["I still think about a young woman named Sarah.","Her name was Sarah. Thirty four years old. Dying from advanced cancer.","When I entered her room, she was sitting up in bed, surrounded by drawings taped to the wall. Crooked suns. Stick figure families. One that just said MRS. K WE MISS YOU in careful, uneven crayon letters."],"posted":"2026-11-16T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["dying"],"part":4,"pz":{"1":1.5}},{"id":"s082","slug":"earl-and-dorothy","title":"Earl and Dorothy","sub":"Sometimes love just needs somewhere to put its hands.","col":"The Wisdom They Share","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":4,"preview":["Some time ago, I walked into a quiet room at an assisted living facility.","I was there to visit a woman named Dorothy, in her final hours.","Her breathing was labored. A soft fluttering sound on each exhale, like a page turning slowly in another room."],"posted":"2026-11-16T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["dying"],"part":2,"pz":{"2":1.5}},{"id":"s083","slug":"marcus","title":"Marcus","sub":"Real strength is owning what you did.","col":"The Wisdom They Share","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":3,"preview":["There is a man named Marcus I still think about.","I was called to see him in his final days.","When I entered his room, he was sitting up in bed, looking out the window. No visitors' chairs pulled close. No get well cards. Just Marcus and the light."],"posted":"2026-11-16T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["dying"],"part":3},{"id":"s084","slug":"walter","title":"Walter","sub":"Learning how to be alone without being lonely.","col":"The Wisdom They Share","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":4,"preview":["A while back, I met a man named Walter.","The room was quiet except for his oxygen machine, ticking on a slow rhythm behind him.","No photographs on the walls. No cards on the windowsill. Just Walter, a chair, and the machine."],"posted":"2026-11-16T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["dying","faith"],"part":1,"pz":{"2":1.5}},{"id":"s085","slug":"off-duty","title":"Off Duty","sub":"Hospice is different when it's one of ours.","col":"New Stories","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":7,"preview":["Kevin wasn't wearing teal.","In almost two years on the same team, I had never seen him out of scrubs. Now he sat in the visitor's chair in a gray hoodie, elbows on his knees, car keys still in his fist like he hadn't decided whether to stay.","His father lay in the bed by the window, turned toward the glass. He didn't look at me. He didn't look at anything."],"posted":"","book":1,"topics":["dying","kids"]},{"id":"s129","slug":"one-wish","title":"One Wish","sub":"A deck of cards, a little Lawrence Welk, and Amazing Grace","col":"New Stories","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":8,"preview":["My friend David has one wish. He told me about it over a game of War.","David has lived in a group home his whole adult life. Nobody told him that meant he couldn’t live a full one, and if they did, he didn’t listen. He loves baseball, basketball, and bowling. He sings. He plays guitar. He dances. Some days we dance to Big Band music together until we’re both red in the face.","A year ago, I was the chaplain and David was the patient. We read Scripture and prayed, and that was most of it. We still read and pray. Now we do it as friends. Besides the hospice team, I’m the only visitor David has anymore.","He’s always in the same spot when I arrive. Right between the activity room and the TV room, where the traffic is heaviest and something is always happening. He picks that spot on purpose. David loves people.","“Hey David! What’s up, buddy?”","“Hi, Chaplain Chris! How are you?”","“I’m good. How are you?”","“I’m doing okay.” He leans in. “Say. What do you wanna do today?”","“How about some cards?”","“Yeah!”","I set his feet on the footrests and we roll toward his room. At the doorway, he sings it out like he does every time.","“Here we are!”","His room looks the way it usually does. Clothes piled on the one chair. Odds and ends stacked on the one table. Bed unmade. So I tidy up a little. Fold the clothes, clear the chair, wipe down the table. Friends don’t leave friends in a messy room.","Then I sit down and give him my full attention.","“So what are we playing? Crazy Eights? Go Fish? War?”"],"posted":"2026-11-04T21:08:40.623Z","topics":["faith"]},{"id":"s130","slug":"time-of-your-life","title":"Time of Your Life","sub":"The wrong song, the right room, and a family that laughed until it cried.","col":"New Stories","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":8,"preview":["I agreed to lead Ray’s funeral before I knew anything about him except his name.","I met him on the last morning of his life. His family called me to their small apartment, and I climbed the stairs into a room that was hot, crowded, and holding its breath. I made it inside just moments before he died.","There was no time to settle in or learn anyone’s story. There was only the work in front of me. The tears. The heavy grief. The hands that needed holding."],"posted":"2026-11-04T21:09:34.016Z","book":1,"topics":["grief"]},{"id":"s131","slug":"beyond-six-months","title":"Beyond Six Months","sub":"","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":7,"preview":["Hospice has a front door with a number on it. A doctor looks at the chart, does the math, and decides a person might have six months or less. That is the ticket in.","But people are not math. Some patients read the fine print, shrug, and keep living for a year, two years, once in a while three. Some stabilize, choose treatment again, and graduate. We really do call it that. Hospice may be the only school where the goal is to get kicked out.","And then there are the long ones. The patients you see so many times that the visits stop feeling like visits and start feeling like a standing appointment with a friend. I lost one of those this week, and it hit my heart differently."],"posted":"2026-11-16T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["dying","grief","faith"]},{"id":"s132","slug":"i-can-use-both","title":"I Can Use Both","sub":"When a patient’s story starts to feel like my own","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":8,"preview":["Sometimes I have so much in common with a patient and their family that the lines start to blur, and their story starts to feel like mine.","Brian was one of those.","His wife answered the door. She was about my wife’s age, with a warm smile and the kind of tired you can see from across the room. Tired in the body, and tired somewhere deeper."],"posted":"2026-11-11T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["dying","faith"]},{"id":"s133","slug":"tell-them-i-love-them","title":"Tell Them I Love Them","sub":"Hospice enters the last chapter of a long story","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":9,"preview":["Sometimes I’m reminded that hospice steps into a story a family has been carrying for a long time. We show up for the final chapter, but the family has been living every chapter before it, sometimes for years.","And sometimes those years have been a nightmare.","Mike’s family was one of those."],"posted":"2026-11-16T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["dying","faith"]},{"id":"s134","slug":"what-do-you-have-left","title":"What Do You Have Left?","sub":"A visit with Stan, and the words that still come","col":"Published on Substack","status":"posted","open":false,"substack":"","credit":"Story: Chris Joy, from my notebook. Names and details changed.","mins":10,"preview":["Some visits sting for a few hours. Today was one of them. It was also one of the most beautiful visits I’ve had in a long time, and I’ve learned those two things often come together.","When a visit stays with me like this, I write it down.","So this is Stan."],"posted":"2026-11-16T15:00:00.000Z","book":1,"topics":["faith"]}],"lock":{"iv":"KO+KG4tN7ro4Wqyn","ct":""},"letters":[],"cards":[],"ws":"kJv6Og3Omdt+fOhNNDoftA==","wraps":[{"c":"mt1r3d8","iv":"+osOWdg9Z/e6ispw","ct":"aTLwqtt4NIuU1A7Z1ch4L/hj/fEfCnXa+Zm+3EQ+6kO4VDePYfSq25zr+jf4Afoq"},{"c":"m1rx99b","iv":"6a8Yj4woKIo3wPkE","ct":"4jbPhHlZTSBTB+Qun4VCV5qlA5LrDd2Qiagi+mTn7SoZ8CQdAcDYk0PumPjho6Qo"},{"c":"mg453qo","iv":"yC3G/vnGHyCnrhNe","ct":"9LVczYpWU1JAYzDmAiQJSf7yNTVOlHv3jNsG3bs/25diKdLmI7qV/FSeSNzQ+C/1"},{"c":"m1hj1vu","iv":"Cyr1j3mXaZkxsrep","ct":"vjo/S92lI+YI89ZehBohTsaWBuSs/gbJ0uCLl/da7kn/j9oSarW9oareNBVLniEc"},{"c":"m5epfi1","iv":"jogld6lBJB85bdZf","ct":"I6F+31Xx8HnnZhSlPwCj/24Vl+xPbvP4xni0LInhk6VvCpbxhX/p3QS8G0wT5mc1"},{"c":"m3xuj16","iv":"gW1vQr+624iO2y6p","ct":"1Ua0X9pO1yZkqedp4EHeTCGJ49xh/baZOUXhzozgD2aXhZQdz2+4EU4JmMhfyeqA"},{"c":"mcegh59","iv":"lI5t8mmwknX/wtCK","ct":"HmOjksbG7c6lInbMuBWiTw6sjZh1gzaTaHIb/QrsesYZ8GIfYXnUlt8XGVucMnvu"},{"c":"m0ce2ax","iv":"YZ91rHdbNx7Bijph","ct":"GVUFKTNvJzkcQ5uyfzRfD/pthIL4TWYLsXygGuQ/KtHo38B0H69Z37R8aSNCYNqH"}]};
